{"name":"Pacific Panorama","hex":"#c0d6ea","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pacific","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Calm, peaceful.","Preferring peace by nature; avoiding violence."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pacific"},{"term":"panorama","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An unbroken view of an entire surrounding area.","A picture or series of pictures representing a continuous scene."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/panorama"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pacific Ocean","blocks":[{"text":"The Pacific Ocean is the largest and deepest of Earth's five oceanic divisions. It stretches from the Arctic Ocean in the north to the Southern Ocean, or, depending on the definition, to Antarctica in the south, and is bounded by the continents of Asia and Australia in the west and the Americas in the east."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Pacific_Ocean_-_en.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacific_Ocean","wikidata":"Q98"},{"title":"Panorama","blocks":[{"text":"A panorama is any wide-angle view or representation of a physical space, whether in painting, drawing, photography, film, seismic images, or 3D modeling. The word was coined in the 18th century by the English painter Robert Barker to describe his panoramic paintings of Edinburgh and London. The motion-picture term panning is derived from panorama."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/360%C2%B0_interior_panorama_of_San_Gregorio_church_in_Breglia%2C_Italy.jpg/330px-360%C2%B0_interior_panorama_of_San_Gregorio_church_in_Breglia%2C_Italy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panorama","wikidata":"Q41363"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Fijian_double_canoe%2C_model%2C_Otago_Muse